---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '@esa/docs/base';
import Section from '@esa/docs/Section.astro';

// Tier 3 — the surface a spoke reaches. All three live together in
// component-tokens.css under FOCUS RING.
const tokens = [
  { token: '--focus-ring-color', maps: '--color-border-default-focus', role: 'The ring. Your brand, at the step that can be seen.' },
  { token: '--focus-ring-width', maps: '--border-width-focus', role: 'Thickness. 2px.' },
  { token: '--focus-ring-offset', maps: '2px literal', role: 'The gap between the element and the ring. A separated ring reads as its own mark rather than a thicker border.' },
];

const criteria = [
  { sc: '2.4.7 Focus Visible', level: 'AA', asks: 'A visible indicator exists for anything that takes keyboard focus.', us: 'Every focusable element paints the ring. check-a11y blocks removal at write time — including the unconditional `.thing { outline: none }` shape that the guard missed until 2026-08-16.' },
  { sc: '1.4.11 Non-Text Contrast', level: 'AA', asks: 'The indicator contrasts 3:1 with adjacent colours.', us: 'The ring is the first step of the brand ramp clearing 3:1 on every surface. The hub default lands on grass step 10: 3.91:1 raised, canvas and floating, 3.07:1 sunken. The ring has a SECOND colour — red, on an invalid field — and it is held to the same bar: red-9 at 3.43:1 worst. check-contrast.mjs blocks on all four surfaces for BOTH colours, in both schemes, per theme.' },
  { sc: '2.4.13 Focus Appearance', level: 'AAA', asks: 'Contrasting area at least as large as a 2px perimeter of the element.', us: 'One 2px band outside the box, which meets the 2px perimeter. The inset rings are flush 2px, which matches it exactly.' },
  { sc: '2.4.11 / 2.4.12 Focus Not Obscured', level: 'AA / AAA', asks: 'The focused element is not hidden behind other content.', us: 'focus.css sets scroll-margin-block-start from --focus-scroll-margin. Any layout with sticky chrome must set it; the docs shell sets 76px for its 56px bar.' },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Focus — Ecology" activeHref="/foundations/focus">
  <nav class="crumbs"><a href={withBase('/')}>Ecology</a> <span>/</span> <span>Foundations</span> <span>/</span> <span>Focus</span></nav>
  <h1>Focus</h1>
  <p class="lede">The focus indicator is the keyboard user's cursor. It is the one visual state you never get to remove — and the one place where a brand colour is not automatically allowed to be itself, because a colour chosen for brand reasons cannot promise to be visible.</p>

  <Section id="ring" title="The ring is your brand, at a step that can be seen">
    <p>
      Tab through anything on this site. The ring is <strong>one 2px band</strong>, 2px clear of the
      element, and it is the brand — just not always the brand's <em>fill</em> step.
    </p>
    <div class="stage">
      <button class="demo">Tab to me</button>
      <a class="demo demo--link" href="#ring">…and me</a>
      <input class="demo demo--input" aria-label="Demo field" placeholder="…and me" />
    </div>
    <p>
      Here is the problem that shapes everything else on this page. Measured on 2026-08-16, a ring
      taken straight from the brand fill managed <strong>2.95:1</strong> against the raised surface
      and <strong>2.66:1</strong> against the sunken one — both short of the 3:1 that SC 1.4.11
      requires. And that is not a bad colour choice to be corrected: whatever a spoke picks for its
      brand is picked for <em>brand</em> reasons and can land anywhere on the luminance scale. So
      <strong>"the ring is the brand" and "the ring always clears 3:1" cannot both be guaranteed by
      one flat value.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      The reason the fill step in particular falls short is worth naming, because it looks like it
      should work: a Radix step 9 is engineered to clear 3:1 as a <strong>solid fill</strong>
      carrying text. A focus ring needs 3:1 as a <strong>2px hairline</strong> against the same
      surfaces. Same number, harder job.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the ring <strong>walks the brand's own ramp</strong> — step 9, then 10, 11, 12 — and takes
      the first step that clears 3:1 on every surface in that scheme. The hub's own green moves one
      step, from <code>grass-9</code> to <code>grass-10</code>, and lands at 3.07:1 on the sunken
      surface. Both shipped themes, beacon and qanat, already clear 3:1 at step 9 and are
      <em>untouched</em>. A brand that works is never moved.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Swept across 216 brand seeds in both schemes, every ramp had a usable step — the worst
      best-available was 9.55:1. Step 12 of a light ramp is dark and step 12 of a dark ramp is
      light, so the far end of the curve is always usable. There is a neutral fallback underneath
      for a ramp with no usable step, and in practice it never fires: <strong>the ring is always the
      brand.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>second, non-brand contrast band</strong> — the accessibility literature's universal
      focus indicator, pairing a light and a dark outline so one always contrasts — was tried on
      2026-08-16 and reverted the same day: it read as a heavy near-black slab around every focused
      control. Do not re-add a band without agreeing the requirement and the weight first. Its three
      tokens were never shipped.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="tokens" title="The three tokens">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Maps to</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {tokens.map((t) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{t.token}</code></td>
            <td><code>{t.maps}</code></td>
            <td>{t.role}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>--color-border-default-focus</code> is where the walk's answer lands, and it names a
      brand <em>ramp step</em> rather than the brand role. It used to chain off
      <code>--color-background-brand</code>, and giving that up was the price of the guarantee: a
      <code>var()</code> chain that always resolves cannot express <em>"the brand, unless the brand
      cannot be seen"</em>. CSS has no function that tests a candidate colour's contrast — Color 5's
      <code>color-contrast()</code> was cut down to <code>contrast-color()</code>, which only returns
      black or white against a background — so the condition cannot live in the cascade at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three things replace what the chain used to guarantee, so a spoke still cannot end up with the
      hub's hue: <code>theme-recipe.mjs</code> emits this role for <strong>every</strong> generated
      theme in both schemes, even when the walk picks step 9 and the value does not change; the spoke
      template carries the declaration so a hand-authored theme has it in front of it; and
      <code>check-contrast.mjs</code> grades it at <code>fail</code> against all four surfaces in both
      schemes. Teeth in the gate, not the cascade.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>A spoke should not declare <code>--color-border-default-focus</code> by hand.</strong>
      A theme file loads after <code>tokens.css</code> at equal specificity, so declaring it overrides
      the walk and hands back the failure it exists to prevent. Let
      <code>make-theme.mjs</code> pick it.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>One ground no brand-derived ring can serve.</strong> A knockout app bar is near-black
      in the <em>light</em> scheme, so a ring dark enough for near-white surfaces is nearly invisible
      on it — measured 2026-08-17, qanat 2.82:1 and the spoke template 2.54:1, neither caused nor
      fixed by the walk. That is what the tier-3 hook is for: a component on dark ground re-points
      <code>--focus-ring-color</code> locally, as <code>esa-button variant="chrome"</code> already
      does by taking it from <code>currentColor</code>. The gate carries it as a
      <code>warn</code> rather than pretending a single token could fix it.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="error" title="On an invalid field the ring turns red">
    <p>
      <strong>Ten</strong> components have an error state, and each re-points
      <strong>one token</strong> on its error wrapper:
    </p>
    <pre class="code" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Error ring rule"><code>{`.field--error {
  --focus-ring-color: var(--form-error-border-color);
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Same band, same width, same offset — only the colour moves. That is the whole rule, and
      re-pointing the <em>token</em> rather than overriding <code>outline-color</code> is
      deliberate: <strong>a field is not one focusable thing.</strong>
      <code>esa-combobox</code> has five things that read the ring colour — an input, a text
      trigger, a field trigger, the dropdown's own search box, and every chip remove button.
      <code>esa-checkbox-group</code> has N boxes, <code>esa-radio-group</code> N circles, and
      <code>esa-form-field</code> does not own its control at all. A property override means one
      rule per focusable part, and every part you miss keeps ringing brand-green inside a field
      that is telling the user it is invalid. Custom properties <strong>inherit</strong>, so one
      declaration reaches all of them — including slotted light-DOM content, and across a shadow
      boundary, which is the only channel that works in every engine.
    </p>
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Error wrapper</th><th>What the ring used to be</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>esa-text-field</code>, <code>esa-textarea</code>, <code>esa-input-tag</code></td><td><code>.field--error</code></td><td>a red <code>box-shadow</code>, stacked as a second band</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>esa-select</code>, <code>esa-combobox</code>, <code>esa-date-picker</code></td><td><code>.field--error</code></td><td>a <code>box-shadow</code> in <strong>red-6</strong> — 1.40:1, near-invisible</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>esa-form-field</code></td><td><code>.esa-form-field--error</code></td><td>nothing at all — a brand ring on an invalid field</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>esa-checkbox-group</code>, <code>esa-radio-group</code></td><td><code>.items--error</code></td><td>nothing at all</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>esa-button-toggle</code></td><td><code>.group--error</code></td><td>nothing at all</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>A dropdown panel inside the field inherits this too.</strong>
      <code>esa-select</code> and <code>esa-combobox</code> both render their panel inside
      <code>.container</code>, so the search box in an invalid combobox's dropdown rings red as
      well. That is consistent — it is all one field — but it is not something you would predict
      from reading the rule, so it is written down here as a decision rather than left to be
      discovered.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>The property-override version broke, and that is why the token is the mechanism.</strong>
      This rule was originally a <code>box-shadow</code>, back when the base rule painted the
      ring with <code>box-shadow</code> too — a true override. On 2026-08-16 the forced-colors
      pass converted the base rule to <code>outline</code> and left this one behind. The two
      rules no longer shared a property, so the override became an <em>addition</em>: a focused
      invalid field painted <strong>three concentric bands in two colours</strong> — red border,
      red shadow flush to it, a 2px gap, then the brand outline. Nobody designed that.
      <code>check-a11y</code> could not see it either, because its ring check asks whether a
      ring is <em>present</em>, and two rings pass that as easily as one. Re-pointing the token
      cannot fail that way: there is no second property to fall out of step with.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the ring has two colours, <strong>both</strong> are gated.
      <code>--form-error-border-color</code> is graded against the same four surfaces at the
      same 3:1, at <code>fail</code> level — red-9 measures 3.43:1 on the worst surface, and
      red-11 under the assurance profile measures 4.57:1.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Not <code>--color-border-utility-danger</code>.</strong> Three of the six painted
      this ring from that role until 2026-08-17, and it is <strong>red-6</strong> — a subtle
      <em>border</em> step, measuring <strong>1.40:1</strong> on a sunken surface, with a
      literal fallback of <code>rgba(211, 47, 47, 0.25)</code> at 1.26:1. Three components
      shipped an error ring that was very nearly invisible, and nothing measured it because
      the ring had no gated row at all. It is still the right token for
      <code>esa-error-summary</code>'s 1px panel border, which is what a step 6 is for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note what the red ring is <em>not</em> doing: carrying the error. WCAG requires the error
      be identified <strong>in text</strong> (SC 3.3.1) and forbids colour as the only signal
      (SC 1.4.1), and the components already satisfy both —
      <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the input, the message linked by
      <code>aria-describedby</code>, and the visible error text. The red ring is a design
      choice on top of a contract that is already met, which is why it is allowed to be a
      colour change and nothing more.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="writing" title="Writing a focus rule">
    <p>The house shape, and the only one you should need:</p>
    {/* tabindex="0" because the block scrolls horizontally: a scrollable region that
        cannot take focus cannot be scrolled by keyboard at all. aria-label because
        making it focusable means it now needs a name when it is landed on. */}
    <pre class="code" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Focus rule example"><code>{`.thing:focus-visible {
  outline: var(--focus-ring-width) solid var(--focus-ring-color);
  outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
}`}</code></pre>
    <p class="note">
      This sample carried a fourth line until 2026-08-17 —
      <code>box-shadow: 0 0 0 var(--focus-ring-halo-spread) var(--focus-ring-halo)</code> — and
      neither of those tokens was ever declared. A <code>var()</code> with nothing to resolve to and
      no fallback is invalid at computed-value time, so the declaration was discarded silently, with
      no console warning. Anyone who copied this shape wrote a dead line. Worth knowing as a failure
      mode, not just as a correction.
    </p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>
        <strong><code>outline</code> for the ring, never <code>box-shadow</code>.</strong>
        Forced-colors modes replace backgrounds, borders and box-shadows with system colours;
        outlines survive. Fourteen components painted their ring with box-shadow alone until
        2026-08-16 — every form control we ship — which meant the entire form layer had no
        keyboard indicator in Windows High Contrast Mode. <code>outline</code> has followed
        <code>border-radius</code> in every engine since 2021, so the rounded ring that motivated
        box-shadow is no longer a reason.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>:focus-visible</code>, not <code>:focus</code></strong> — the ring appears
        for keyboard and programmatic focus but not on a mouse click. Two exceptions, both
        deliberate: text entry uses <code>:focus-within</code> on the field wrapper (a ring on
        click is native behaviour for something you are about to type into), and
        <code>esa-error-summary</code> uses bare <code>:focus</code> because a user <em>sent</em>
        there by a failed submit did not click anything and <code>:focus-visible</code> heuristics
        can decline to paint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never a bare <code>outline: none</code>.</strong> If the ring belongs somewhere
        else — a chromeless input inside a bordered row — put a <code>:focus-within</code> ring on
        the row and say so in a comment. The reset on its own is a
        <a href={withBase('/guide/toolkit')}>check-a11y</a> block.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inset the ring only when the box is clipped.</strong>
        <code>outline-offset: calc(var(--focus-ring-offset) * -1)</code>, for a full-bleed row
        inside an <code>overflow: hidden</code>
        panel. A flush 2px inner ring has the same area as a 2px perimeter, so it still meets
        Focus Appearance — but an outward ring is easier to see and should be the default.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="criteria" title="What each criterion asks, and how we meet it">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th>Success criterion</th><th>Level</th><th>Asks for</th><th>How we meet it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {criteria.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{c.sc}</strong></td>
            <td>{c.level}</td>
            <td>{c.asks}</td>
            <td>{c.us}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>axe does not evaluate focus indicators at all.</strong> <code>npm run a11y</code>
      stayed green through every failure on this page, including the <strong>four</strong> components
      that had deleted their ring outright — <code>esa-entity-search</code>,
      <code>esa-search-panel</code>, <code>esa-command-palette</code> and <code>esa-combobox</code>,
      each carrying an unconditional <code>outline: none</code> on a base class, and all four the
      search input a keyboard user lands on first. It also cannot see a contrast ratio, which is why
      the 3:1 guarantee above is enforced by <code>check-contrast.mjs</code> and not by axe. The test
      is a keyboard and your eyes.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="light-dom" title="Light DOM vs shadow DOM">
    <p>
      A stylesheet cannot cross a shadow boundary, so the ring is delivered twice from one source
      of truth.
    </p>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>
        <strong>Light DOM</strong> — <code>@esa/tokens/focus.css</code>, imported by the spoke
        template's <code>BaseLayout</code>. It rings every <code>a, button, summary, input,
        select, textarea, [tabindex]</code> at zero specificity (<code>:where()</code>), so a
        component rule always wins. It also carries the <code>:focus:not(:focus-visible)</code>
        undo, which is what suppresses the browser's own ring only where ours replaces it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shadow DOM</strong> — each Lit component paints its own from the same three tokens
        (custom properties inherit through the boundary). The shared
        <code>@esa/tokens/a11y.css</code> is bridged in via <code>a11y.ts</code> and carries the
        forced-colors fallback, so a component listing <code>a11y</code> in
        <code>static styles</code> keeps a ring even where its own colours are overridden.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Until 2026-08-16 the only correct page-level focus rule in the repo lived in the docs
      shell — a file spokes never receive. Every light-DOM component we ship had a tokenised ring
      here and the browser default everywhere else.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: var(--font-size-400, 1.125rem); color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #525252); max-width: 62ch; }
  .stage {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--spacing-500, 1.5rem);
    padding: var(--spacing-600, 2rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f0f0f0);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md, 0.5rem);
    margin-block: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
  }
  .demo {
    font: inherit;
    padding: var(--spacing-250, 0.625rem) var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default, #e5e5e5);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md, 0.5rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-raised, #fff);
    color: var(--color-content-default, #171717);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .demo--link { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
  .demo--input { cursor: text; }
  .tbl { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-block: var(--spacing-400, 1rem); font-size: var(--font-size-200, 0.875rem); }
  .tbl th, .tbl td { text-align: left; padding: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem) var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default, #e5e5e5); vertical-align: top; }
  .tbl th { color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #525252); font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold, 550); }
  .code {
    padding: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f0f0f0);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md, 0.5rem);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .rules li { margin-block-end: var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem); line-height: var(--line-height-relaxed, 1.8); max-width: 72ch; }
  .note {
    padding: var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem) var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    border-inline-start: var(--border-width-200, 2px) solid var(--color-border-default, #e5e5e5);
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #525252);
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
</style>
